7592186551258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551258 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551258 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344058" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551258 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551258 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}-3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}} & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551258 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "336eb7d8-27df-584e-adc9-df5d1de8bc4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551258 #edb/ref 1074 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,-3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,=-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,=-3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the coefficients next to sines and cosines, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-3 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n}=\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}-3{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=2,$ which means the constant term is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{9}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{9})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}-3{a}_{n}=\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=-\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{9}\\cdot (-\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}∼1 & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{162}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & +{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+81)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551307 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551307 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551307 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551307 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551307 #edb/ref 1071 "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+3y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{{field-2}}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551307 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a503430-335a-52ca-a8f2-1a25c8b18de5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551307 #edb/ref 1074 "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{2}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{2}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & \\,+3(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{3{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{a}_{n})cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{b}_{n})sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{a}_{0}}{2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{a}_{n}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{2}+3{b}_{n}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the second equation, \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the third equation gives\n\n$$\n-(-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{6})\\frac{n\\pi }{2}+3{b}_{n}=\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${a}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{n\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,-\\frac{4(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{24(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+36)}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551352 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186551152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551352 #edb/ref 1069 "q-343906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551352 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551352 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x),$ where the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{9}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551352 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f846bdbb-b4eb-53cc-9341-26d00f9d6239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186551352 #edb/ref 1074 "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $2$-periodic function $(L=1)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{1}=n\\pi .\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\pi .$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))+x(-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+{\\pi }^{2}y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x):$\n\n$$\n[-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)-{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+{\\pi }^{2}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-2\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi A=9 \\\\ 2\\pi B=0\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=-\\frac{9}{2\\pi } \\\\ B=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=-\\frac{9x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
